            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

We had identified that your house is pretty air tight already and does rely on those 2 x high wall passive air vents outside the master bedroom &amp; central bathroom. As the house requires fresh air (primarily when a bathroom fan or the clothes dryer is on), outside fresh air is brought in through those 2 vents. You can adjust the vent openings by adjusting the "diffuser cones" in or out. When I arrived at your house, both fresh air supply vents were fully closed.



Although your house is currently quite air tight, it will become even tighter in conjunction with a couple of upgrades we talked about:



** Replacing the front main entrance and rear entrance doors (or at least adjusting the current ones and installing new weather-stripping)



** The 6" chimney &amp; 4" combustion air supply pipe will be eliminated when you install a new high efficiency combi boiler.



Savings: There should be about a 10% reduction in overall air leakage that, because your house is energy tight already, will likely be offset by the additional ventilation you will need to provide, perhaps by operating a bathroom fan a bit more or perhaps if you choose to install a Heat Recovery Ventilation unit (see that section of my report). 



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the 10% reduction I have modeled.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors>

You mentioned that you will either adjust &amp; upgrade the weather-stripping of the 2 existing exterior doors of the house (front main entrance &amp; rear entrance), or you may choose to upgrade them to new ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. If you replace them, I recommend doors with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/m??????K or an Energy Rating of a minimum 34.



Savings:

By replacing these 2 entry door systems with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 12 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $487.50 in rebates (3 door units @ $162.50 each) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system (includes all windows in the door, sidelights and transom windows above) is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</doors>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, there appeared to be minimal ventilation for your home. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality. See the Additional Comments section of this report where I talk about this further, including my comments on the "Passive Ventilation" system currently installed in your house.



I recommend that you consider having an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. I recommend you install one that is a minimum 75% efficient and has an ECM circulation motor to keep operation costs low. Perhaps a system designer can find a creative way to integrate this HRV into the existing bedroom supply pipe network that is currently in place ?



Savings:

By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, this will increase the ventilation of your house (which is required), with an estimated $ 99 increase per year in energy costs, increasing your annual energy usage by 2.4 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

I have modeled a combination heating &amp; hot water system for this house - please see the recommendation in the Heating System section of this report.



</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is heated with a combination oil boiler that has a connection (a combi loop) to also heat your domestic hot water, with a 182 litre external storage buffer tank (an electric tank). The boiler appears to be original to when the house was built, and you mentioned that you have not been using the combi loop &amp; instead just using the electric hot water tank (in all electric mode) year round. Perhaps the "water jacket" heat exchanger internal to the boiler is no longer functioning ?



As per our discussion, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water. This system will direct vent to the outside and will no longer require the 6" chimney or the 4" combustion air supply pipe.



NOTE: You may want to work with the provider of your new boiler to design an in-floor radiant heat distribution system for your main floor, rather than just having the one exterior perimeter loop that currently exists ?



Savings:

By upgrading to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water, you could potentially save $ 3,588 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 59 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an older oil or propane fired boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified propane fired boiler of the 95% efficiency that I modeled. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Renewable Energy Systems



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 5 kW PV system directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. Based on available roof space with a decent solar exposure, I split the 5 kW into 2 x 2.5 kW systems:

1. 2.5 kW flush mounted on the front facing roof of the house (Southwest)

2. 2.5 kW flush mounted on the right facing roof of the garage (Southeast)



This is estimated to produce about 4,732 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,588, and qualify for the maximum $5,000 from the Canada Greener Homes program.

</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



????????? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

????????? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

????????? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

????????? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

????????? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

????????? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (for your house, the 2 bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 8.3 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current wood pellet stove is power vented (with a dedicated combustion air supply), however the boiler is chimney vented and is susceptible to combustion back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed a combo smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detector on the main floor, however none in the basement where thew boiler is located. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood (does not vent to outside)

        - 2 bathroom fans

        - The clothes dryer

        - A passive "make-up" air system with 2 x high wall vents in the central part of the house



If you choose to replace any of the above ventilation components, please select ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. For the new bathroom fans, you may want to retain the existing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Dec. 1, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.40 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.25 per liter 

Propane - $1.00 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.99 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



